    48	    its tip as the farthest reachable point.
    49	  - Fused logic here: infer direction from black to red, test only (3,2), (3,3), ..., and stop
    50	    at (3,7) because the next straight step would overlap the teal obstacle. The branch is ignored.
    51	- Difference from the original task:
    52	  - original blocker source: river narrows below width n,
    53	  - fused blocker source: river stays straight and readable, but explicit obstacle color occupies
    54	    the forbidden cells.
    55	
    56	b) Complexity calibration
    57	-------------------------
    58	- A plain line with one obstacle would be too weak: the original BFS solver would often produce
    59	  the same output, so the D1 change would not really matter.
    60	- To keep the fusion visible, each generated sample contains:
    61	  - one straight main strip aimed from black toward red,
    62	  - one mandatory side branch before the first blocking obstacle, long enough that BFS would
    63	    prefer its tip over the straight stop,
    64	  - one or more extra teal obstacles elsewhere on the river geometry.
    65	- Freedom is intentionally small:
    66	  - orientation: horizontal or vertical,
    67	  - direction: positive or negative,
    68	  - square size: n in {2,3,4},
    69	  - 1 to 2 side branches,
    70	  - 2 to 4 teal bars total.
    71	- That is enough variation for ARC while keeping the rule legible from a few examples.
    72	
    73	c) Hidden-assumption audit of the original main()
    74	-------------------------------------------------
    75	- Original "sort same-sized squares by color" is replaced. The fused solver uses a visible rule:
    76	  the destination touches a border, the mover does not.
    77	- Original "most common remaining color is river" still holds, but now only because the generator
    78	  guarantees blue area > teal obstacle area. The solver therefore treats every remaining color
    79	  after the largest one as obstacle.
    80	- Original feasible-window BFS is no longer the motion rule:
    81	  - it assumes turning is allowed,
    82	  - that is false after D1/0.
    83	- Original fallback "pick farthest BFS position" is invalid:
    84	  - side branches would attract the mover,
    85	  - the fused rule must ignore them and stay on the black->red ray.
    86	- New solver assumptions introduced here:
    87	  - exactly one repeated-size pair of salient solid squares exists,
    88	  - among that pair, exactly one square touches an edge,
    89	  - mover and destination align horizontally or vertically in top-left placement coordinates,
    90	  - blue is the largest non-(bg/squares) color and any extra remaining color is an obstacle,
    91	  - mover's start position is a valid n x n placement on the passable mask.
    92	- The generator explicitly enforces all of these.
    93	
    94	d) Placement legality
    95	---------------------
    96	- Generation is analytical, not search-heavy:
    97	  - pick the straight main strip first,
    98	  - choose the stop position on that strip,
    99	  - place the first teal bar exactly one step beyond the desired stop,
   100	  - add a longer side branch before that blocker,
   101	  - optionally add a second branch and extra teal bars farther away.
   102	- Every river segment is axis-aligned and painted before squares are placed.
   103	- Teal bars are always 1-pixel thick lines crossing an n-wide strip, so they block n x n windows
   104	  but never masquerade as same-sized solid squares.
   105	- Branches and extra barriers are only added when there is enough space, so they stay in-bounds
   106	  and do not overlap the mover or destination.
   107	
   108	e) Visual observability
   109	-----------------------
   110	- From a few examples, a human can see:
   111	  - black and red are same-sized squares aligned on one straight corridor,
   112	  - teal blocks are explicit obstacles inside the blue track,
   113	  - extra side branches exist but the output still stays on the straight line,
   114	  - the black square stops just before the first teal blocker on that ray.
   115	- No invisible ordering is needed to infer the rule. Edge-touching red gives the motion target,
   116	  and the first teal bar on that axis explains the stop.
   117	
   118	f) Visual cleanliness
   119	---------------------
   120	- Only five colors are used.
   121	- Blue geometry is rectilinear and sparse: one main strip, one required branch, optional extra
   122	  branch, and a small number of teal bars.
   123	- Obstacles never stack on top of each other, and they stay embedded inside blue strips rather
   124	  than scattered arbitrarily across the background.
   125	
   126	g) Change inferability
   127	----------------------
   128	- The only output recoloring is still simple and structural:
   129	  - the black start square becomes blue,
   130	  - black reappears at the terminal straight-ray placement,
   131	  - red stays visible because the generated samples are deliberately blocked before the goal.
   132	- The important inference is geometric, not numeric: "follow the straight shot toward red until the
   133	  first explicit obstacle on that shot."
   134	
   135	h) Anti-degeneration metric used after implementation
   136	-----------------------------------------------------
   137	- `branch_divergence`: compare the fused straight-ray stop with the old BFS-style stop computed on
   138	  the same obstacle-aware traversable mask. The generator requires them to differ.
   139	- `obstacle_effect`: compare the fused stop with the straight-ray stop obtained when teal is treated
   140	  as passable. The generator requires them to differ too.
   141	- If either metric is false, the sample has collapsed into an uninteresting subcase and is rejected.
   142	
   143	i) Post-edit audit of new main() assumptions
   144	--------------------------------------------
   145	- `_pick_roles(...)` assumes one repeated-size pair has one edge-touching and one interior member.
   146	  The generator creates exactly that pattern.
   147	- `_ray_direction(...)` assumes axis alignment. The generator always places red on the same row or
   148	  column as black in placement coordinates.
   149	- `_scene_info(...)` assumes at least two non-excluded colors remain and blue dominates them. The
   150	  generator always paints blue first and keeps teal area smaller.
   151	- `_ray_terminal(...)` assumes the start window is feasible. The generator validates this before
   152	  returning a sample.
   153	"""
   154	
   155	
   156	_BG, _RIVER, _MOVER, _DEST, _OBST = 3, 1, 0, 2, 8
   157	_DIR4 = [(1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)]
   158	
   159	
   160	def _solid_squares(g: np.ndarray, bg: int) -> list[dict]:
   161	    squares: list[dict] = []
   162	    for comp in find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=4, monochromatic=True, return_format="dict"):
   163	        col = int(comp["color"])
   164	        b = comp["bbox"]
   165	        if bbox_h(b) != bbox_w(b):
   166	            continue
   167	        patch = g[int(b[0]) : int(b[2]) + 1, int(b[1]) : int(b[3]) + 1]
   168	        if not bool((patch == int(col)).all()):
   169	            continue
   170	        squares.append({"col": col, "r0": int(b[0]), "c0": int(b[1]), "n": int(bbox_h(b))})
   171	    return squares
   172	
   173	
   174	def _touches_edge(square: dict, H: int, W: int) -> bool:
   175	    r0, c0, n = int(square["r0"]), int(square["c0"]), int(square["n"])
   176	    return r0 == 0 or c0 == 0 or r0 + n == H or c0 + n == W
   177	
   178	
   179	def _edge_gap(square: dict, H: int, W: int) -> int:
   180	    r0, c0, n = int(square["r0"]), int(square["c0"]), int(square["n"])
   181	    return min(r0, c0, H - (r0 + n), W - (c0 + n))
   182	
   183	
   184	def _pick_roles(squares: list[dict], H: int, W: int) -> tuple[dict, dict]:
   185	    by_n: dict[int, list[dict]] = defaultdict(list)
   186	    for sq in squares:
   187	        by_n[int(sq["n"])].append(sq)
   188	
   189	    sizes = [n for n, group in by_n.items() if len(group) >= 2]
   190	    assert sizes
   191	    nside = max(sizes, key=lambda n: (len(by_n[n]), n))
   192	    cand = list(by_n[int(nside)])
   193	
   194	    edge_touching = [sq for sq in cand if _touches_edge(sq, H, W)]
   195	    interior = [sq for sq in cand if not _touches_edge(sq, H, W)]
   196	    assert edge_touching and interior
   197	
   198	    dest = min(edge_touching, key=lambda sq: (_edge_gap(sq, H, W), int(sq["r0"]), int(sq["c0"])))
   199	    mover = min(interior, key=lambda sq: (_edge_gap(sq, H, W), int(sq["r0"]), int(sq["c0"])))
   200	    return mover, dest
   201	
   202	
   203	def _remaining_colors(g: np.ndarray, excluded: set[int]) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:
   204	    counts = Counter(map(int, g.flatten()))
   205	    items = [(int(col), int(ct)) for col, ct in counts.items() if int(col) not in excluded]
   206	    items.sort(key=lambda kv: (-kv[1], kv[0]))
   207	    return items
   208	
   209	
   210	def _scene_info(grid: np.ndarray) -> dict:
   211	    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)
   212	    H, W = g.shape
   213	    bg = int(Counter([int(g[0, 0]), int(g[0, -1]), int(g[-1, 0]), int(g[-1, -1])]).most_common(1)[0][0])
   214	    mover, dest = _pick_roles(_solid_squares(g, bg), H, W)
   215	    rem = _remaining_colors(g, {bg, int(mover["col"]), int(dest["col"])})
   216	    assert len(rem) >= 2
   217	
   218	    river = int(rem[0][0])
   219	    obstacle_colors = {int(col) for col, _ in rem[1:]}
   220	    assert obstacle_colors
   221	
   222	    return {
   223	        "grid": g,
   224	        "H": int(H),
   225	        "W": int(W),
   226	        "bg": int(bg),
   227	        "mover": mover,
   228	        "dest": dest,
   229	        "river": int(river),
   230	        "obstacles": obstacle_colors,
   231	        "n": int(mover["n"]),
   232	        "sr": int(mover["r0"]),
   233	        "sc": int(mover["c0"]),
   234	        "dr": int(dest["r0"]),
   235	        "dc": int(dest["c0"]),
   236	    }
   237	
   238	
   239	def _trav_mask(info: dict, *, obstacle_passable: bool = False) -> np.ndarray:
   240	    g = info["grid"]
   241	    passable = {int(info["river"]), int(info["mover"]["col"]), int(info["dest"]["col"])}
   242	    if obstacle_passable:
   243	        passable |= {int(col) for col in info["obstacles"]}
   244	    return np.isin(g, list(passable))
   245	
   246	
   247	def _window_ok(trav: np.ndarray, r: int, c: int, n: int) -> bool:
   248	    H, W = trav.shape
   249	    if not (0 <= int(r) <= int(H - n) and 0 <= int(c) <= int(W - n)):
   250	        return False
   251	    return bool(trav[int(r) : int(r + n), int(c) : int(c + n)].all())
   252	
   253	
   254	def _ray_direction(sr: int, sc: int, dr: int, dc: int) -> tuple[int, int]:
   255	    if sr == dr and sc != dc:
   256	        return 0, 1 if dc > sc else -1
   257	    if sc == dc and sr != dr:
   258	        return 1 if dr > sr else -1, 0
   259	    raise AssertionError("mover and destination must align on exactly one axis")
   260	
   261	
   262	def _ray_terminal(trav: np.ndarray, sr: int, sc: int, dr: int, dc: int, n: int) -> tuple[int, int]:
   263	    assert _window_ok(trav, sr, sc, n)
   264	    step_r, step_c = _ray_direction(sr, sc, dr, dc)
   265	
   266	    tr, tc = int(sr), int(sc)
   267	    while True:
   268	        nr = int(tr + step_r)
   269	        nc = int(tc + step_c)
   270	        if not _window_ok(trav, nr, nc, n):
   271	            break
   272	        tr, tc = int(nr), int(nc)
   273	        if tr == int(dr) and tc == int(dc):
   274	            break
   275	    return int(tr), int(tc)
   276	
   277	
   278	def _feasible_mask(trav: np.ndarray, n: int) -> np.ndarray:
   279	    H, W = trav.shape
   280	    h2, w2 = H - n + 1, W - n + 1
   281	    feasible = np.zeros((h2, w2), dtype=bool)
   282	    for r in range(h2):
   283	        for c in range(w2):
   284	            feasible[r, c] = bool(trav[r : r + n, c : c + n].all())
   285	    return feasible
   286	
   287	
   288	def _bfs_terminal(trav: np.ndarray, sr: int, sc: int, dr: int, dc: int, n: int) -> tuple[int, int]:
   289	    feasible = _feasible_mask(trav, n)
   290	    assert feasible[int(sr), int(sc)]
   291	
   292	    h2, w2 = feasible.shape
   293	    dist = -np.ones((h2, w2), dtype=int)
   294	    dist[int(sr), int(sc)] = 0
   295	    q = deque([(int(sr), int(sc))])
   296	    while q:
   297	        r, c = q.popleft()
   298	        for dr2, dc2 in _DIR4:
   299	            rr, cc = r + dr2, c + dc2
   300	            if 0 <= rr < h2 and 0 <= cc < w2 and bool(feasible[rr, cc]) and int(dist[rr, cc]) == -1:
   301	                dist[rr, cc] = int(dist[r, c] + 1)
   302	                q.append((rr, cc))
   303	
   304	    if 0 <= dr < h2 and 0 <= dc < w2 and bool(feasible[int(dr), int(dc)]) and int(dist[int(dr), int(dc)]) >= 0:
   305	        return int(dr), int(dc)
   306	
   307	    best = -1
   308	    tr, tc = int(sr), int(sc)
   309	    for r in range(h2):
   310	        for c in range(w2):
   311	            if int(dist[r, c]) > best:
   312	                best = int(dist[r, c])
   313	                tr, tc = int(r), int(c)
   314	    return int(tr), int(tc)
   315	
   316	
   317	def _behavior_metrics(grid: np.ndarray) -> dict:
   318	    info = _scene_info(grid)
   319	    sr, sc = int(info["sr"]), int(info["sc"])
   320	    dr, dc = int(info["dr"]), int(info["dc"])
   321	    n = int(info["n"])
   322	
   323	    trav = _trav_mask(info, obstacle_passable=False)
   324	    trav_open = _trav_mask(info, obstacle_passable=True)
   325	
   326	    ray_stop = _ray_terminal(trav, sr, sc, dr, dc, n)
   327	    bfs_stop = _bfs_terminal(trav, sr, sc, dr, dc, n)
   328	    open_ray_stop = _ray_terminal(trav_open, sr, sc, dr, dc, n)
   329	    return {
   330	        "ray_stop": ray_stop,
   331	        "bfs_stop": bfs_stop,
   332	        "open_ray_stop": open_ray_stop,
   333	        "branch_divergence": ray_stop != bfs_stop,
   334	        "obstacle_effect": ray_stop != open_ray_stop,
   335	        "obstacle_pixels": int((grid == _OBST).sum()),
   336	    }
   337	
